Z||000000||MooCow||10-28-1999||09:10 AM||||I read an article at <A HREF="http://www.cars.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cars.com</A>   for a Fiero and in the article it said that fieros turning radious should be measured in blocks not feet, and that people that own Fiero's dont just zip into parking places because they can't turn sharp enough.  Is that true or just something they made up.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Dave85||10-28-1999||09:34 AM||||Must have been some 90 year old lady writing that article that is used to power steering! I think the turning radius is pretty good. I can turn around using half of the cul-de-sac. I definatly don't have any parking problems, just zip right in there!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-28-1999||09:37 AM||||While the lack of power steering does give some people problems with being able to turn easily and quickly, My Formula turns quite sharp(for u-turns, parking lot, etc.) 1984 through 1987 the Fiero did have a much larger turing diameter than other small cars in its class.  In '88 with the new suspension the Fiero was supposed to turn much sharper.  I've never ridden in a Fiero other than mine, so I don't know what the differences are from experience. The Motorweek video from the Fiero Store goes into a little detail on this subject, but I don't remember the numbers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||10-28-1999||01:26 PM||||There's a significant difference in turning radius between the 88's and the earlier cars.  The 88 has a reasonable turning radius, but the earlier cars' radius is close to 40 feet!  It's just something you have to get used to.  <P>My buddy can turn his Explorer Sport around in less space than I can turn my 86 SE around in.||||reg||13
Z||000004||kornholio||10-28-1999||04:20 PM||||My 86 SE can't even make a full U-turn on a residential street.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula||10-28-1999||07:10 PM||||how to get a short turning radius.<BR>hold in clutch.<BR>Put car in first gear.<BR>keep holding in clutch.<BR>give car gas.<BR>turn and release clutch.<BR>problem solved!<BR>Go ahead and try it.<BR>(Maybe v6 only)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||10-28-1999||07:25 PM||||Amen Formula!  But it has a tendency to wake the neighbors.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tim||10-30-1999||12:14 PM||||The turning radius can be shorten by "adjusting" the stops on the uprights.<P>85-87<BR>Remove a wheel, the front and after you raise the car, turn the steering wheel to lock in the direction of the wheel removed.  Look at the lower shock mount.  The cast iron stub touching can be "adjusted" by grinding it down about a ¼" or 6mm.  Watch tire width.  Brake caliper will need "adjusted" with the steering locked in the other direction.  Shortens the radius about 3' or a meter. <P>Did mine about 5 years ago, made life a little easier.||||reg||1
